:"The Aloha Tower is a retired lighthouse that is considered one of the landmarks of the state of Hawaii in the United States. Opened on September 11, 1926, at a cost of $160,000 ($2,805,206 in 2024), the tower is at Pier 9 of Honolulu Harbor. It is a guiding beacon welcoming vessels to the City and County of Honolulu.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b0/Aloha_Tower_%285651869378%29.jpg/960px-Aloha_Tower_%285651869378%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Aloha Tower"},{"n":"Confucius (551-479 BC)","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":21.312715,"lo":-157.862922,"d":566},{"n":"King Kamehameha V Judiciary History Center","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.30534,"lo":-157.859911,"d":604,"w":"https://www.jhchawaii.net/"},{"n":"Iolani Palace","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.306815,"lo":-157.858766,"d":659,"h":"Tu-Sa 09:00-16:00; PH off","w":"https://www.iolanipalace.org/","ph":"+1 808-522-0822","wp":"en:ʻIolani Palace","wd":"Q423525","ad":"364 South King Street","ds":"The ʻIolani Palace (Hawaiian pronunciation: [ʔi.oˈla.ni]; Hawaiian: Hale Aliʻi ʻIolani) was the royal residence of the rulers of the Kingdom of Hawaiʻi beginning with Kamehameha III under the Kamehameha Dynasty (1845) and ending with Queen Liliʻuokalani (1893) under the Kalākaua Dynasty. It is located in the capitol district of downtown Honolulu in the U.S. state of Hawaiʻi.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8e/Iolani_Palace_%2851872681413%29.jpg/960px-Iolani_Palace_%2851872681413%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"ʻIolani Palace"},{"n":"Capitol Modern","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.308857,"lo":-157.858237,"d":707,"h":"Mo-Sa 10:00-16:00; PH off","w":"https://www.capitolmodern.org/","ph":"+1 808 586 0900","wp":"en:Capitol Modern","wd":"Q122911605","ad":"250 South Hotel Street","f":["wc","free"],"ds":"The Capitol Modern Museum, is a public art museum located on the second floor of the No. 1 Capitol District Building in downtown Honolulu, Hawaii, United States. The museum is operated by the Hawaii State Foundation on Culture and the Arts as a part of their Art in Public Places Program.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/53/No._1_Capitol_District_Building%2C_Honolulu%2C_HI.JPG/960px-No._1_Capitol_District_Building%2C_Honolulu%2C_HI.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Capitol Modern"},{"n":"Dr Sun Yat-sen","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":21.314301,"lo":-157.861798,"d":775},{"n":"Father Damien Statue","g":"sights","k":"artwork","la":21.30762,"lo":-157.856958,"d":834,"wp":"en:Statue of Father Damien","wd":"Q5437586","ds":"The Father Damien Statue, also called the Saint Damien of Molokaʻi Statue, is the centerpiece of the entrance to the Hawaiʻi State Capitol and the Hawaiʻi State Legislature in Honolulu, Hawaiʻi. A second bronze cast is displayed in the National Statuary Hall Collection in the United States Capitol, along with the Kamehameha Statue.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c1/Father_Damien_statue_%2851871640602%29.jpg/960px-Father_Damien_statue_%2851871640602%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Statue of Father Damien"},{"n":"Eternal Flame for 9/11 Victims","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":21.305115,"lo":-157.85754,"d":836,"ds":"Eternal flame for World Trade Center victims September 11, 2001"},{"n":"Replica of Liberty Bell","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":21.307485,"lo":-157.856703,"d":861},{"n":"Washington Place","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.308712,"lo":-157.856723,"d":861,"w":"https://washingtonplace.hawaii.gov/","wp":"en:Washington Place","wd":"Q7972130","ad":"320 Beretania Street","ds":"Washington Place is a Greek Revival palace in the Hawaii Capital Historic District in Honolulu, Hawaii. It was where Queen Liliʻuokalani was arrested during the overthrow of the Hawaiian Kingdom. Later it became the official residence of the governor of Hawaii.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/83/Washington_Place%2C_O%E2%80%98ahu_%282024%29-L1004906.jpg/960px-Washington_Place%2C_O%E2%80%98ahu_%282024%29-L1004906.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Washington Place"},{"n":"Hawaii Mission Houses Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.303794,"lo":-157.856788,"d":970,"h":"Tu-Sa 10:00-16:00","w":"https://www.missionhouses.org/","ph":"+1 808 447 3910","wp":"en:Hawaiian Mission Houses Historic Site and Archives","wd":"Q6878617","ad":"553 South King Street","ds":"The Hawaiian Mission Houses Historic Site and Archives in Honolulu, Hawaii, was established in 1920 by the Hawaiian Mission Children's Society, a private, non-profit organization and genealogical society, on the 100th anniversary of the arrival of the first Christian missionaries in Hawaiʻi.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Mission_Houses_Museum_Front.jpg/960px-Mission_Houses_Museum_Front.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Hawaiian Mission Houses Historic Site and Archives"},{"n":"Foster Mansion Driveway Lamp","g":"sights","k":"ruins","la":21.316896,"lo":-157.857449,"d":1261},{"n":"Honolulu Police Department Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.304064,"lo":-157.851284,"d":1486,"h":"Mo-Fr 09:00-15:00","w":"http://www.honolulupd.org/community/index.php?page=museum","ad":"801 South Beretania Street"},{"n":"Lili'uokalani Botanical Garden","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.319171,"lo":-157.855909,"d":1558,"h":"07:00-17:00; Dec 25 off; Jan 01 off","w":"https://www.honolulu.gov/parks/hbg.html","wp":"en:Liliuokalani Botanical Garden","wd":"Q4895276","ad":"123 North Kuakini Street","f":["free"],"ds":"The Liliʻuokalani Botanical Garden is a city park and young botanical garden located on North Kuakini Street, Honolulu, Hawaiʻi. The garden is 7 acres (28,000 m2). It is one of the Honolulu Botanical Gardens, and open daily without charge, except for Christmas and New Year's Day.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/Lili%27uokalani_Botanical_Garden_-_Honolulu%2C_HI.JPG/960px-Lili%27uokalani_Botanical_Garden_-_Honolulu%2C_HI.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Liliʻuokalani Botanical Garden"},{"n":"Hawaii Children's Discovery Center","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.293692,"lo":-157.862074,"d":1619,"h":"Tu-Fr 09:00-13:00; Sa-Su 10:00-15:00; Mo off","w":"https://discoverycenterhawaii.org/","ph":"+1 808 524 5437","wp":"en:Hawaii Children's Discovery Center","wd":"Q20714306","ad":"111 Ohe Street","f":["ac"],"ds":"Hawaii Children's Discovery Center is a children's museum located near Honolulu, Hawaii's Kaka'ako Waterfront Park. Founded in 1989, the 38,000 sq ft (3,500 m2) museum receives over 200,000 visits from children and their families every year. It is a non-profit organization with tax-exempt status, and a member of the Association of Children's Museums.","wpu":"Hawaii Children's Discovery Center"},{"n":"Paradise Pedals Hawaii","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.293857,"lo":-157.858689,"d":1703,"w":"https://paradisepedals.com/"},{"n":"The Honolulu Museum of Art","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.304185,"lo":-157.848489,"d":1762,"h":"We-Su 10:00-18:00; Fr 10:00-21:00; Jan 01 off; Jul 04 off; Nov Th[4] off, Dec 25 off","w":"https://www.honolulumuseum.org/","ph":"+1-808-532-8700","wp":"en:Honolulu Museum of Art","wd":"Q128316","ad":"900 South Beretania Street","f":["wc"],"ds":"The Honolulu Museum of Art (formerly the Honolulu Academy of Arts) is an art museum in Honolulu, Hawaiʻi. The museum is the largest of its kind in the state, and was founded in 1922 by Anna Rice Cooke. It has one of the largest collections of Asian and Pan-Pacific art in the United States, and since its official opening on April 8, 1927, its collections have grown to more than 55,000 works of art.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f2/Honolulu_Museum_of_Art_logo.svg/960px-Honolulu_Museum_of_Art_logo.svg.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Honolulu Museum of Art"},{"n":"Punch Bowl","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":21.310808,"lo":-157.847875,"d":1801},{"n":"Ala Moana Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":21.28879,"lo":-157.850787,"d":2594},{"n":"Kapena Falls Trailhead","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.324122,"lo":-157.846866,"d":2596},{"n":"Bernice Pauahi Bishop Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.333068,"lo":-157.870753,"d":2850,"w":"https://www.bishopmuseum.org/","wp":"en:Bishop Museum","wd":"Q826520","ad":"1525 Bernice Street","f":["wc","free"],"ds":"The Bernice Pauahi Bishop Museum, designated the Hawaiʻi State Museum of Natural and Cultural History, is a museum of history and science in the historic Kalihi district of Honolulu on the Hawaiian island of Oʻahu. Founded in 1889, it is the largest museum in Hawaiʻi and has the world's largest collection of Polynesian cultural artifacts and natural history specimens.","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Bishop_Museum_2016_C.jpg/960px-Bishop_Museum_2016_C.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","wpu":"Bishop Museum"},{"n":"Magic Island lagoon","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":21.282994,"lo":-157.846791,"d":3360},{"n":"Aloha Sails Waikiki","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.287001,"lo":-157.841212,"d":3394,"h":"Mo-Su 09:00-17:00","w":"https://alohasailswaikiki.com/","ph":"+1-808-427-0017"},{"n":"Island Splash Tours","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.282554,"lo":-157.840762,"d":3783,"w":"https://www.islandsplashtours.com","ph":"+1 808-404-3545"},{"n":"Waikiki Starlight Luau","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":21.28287,"lo":-157.838295,"d":3932},{"n":"The Liljestrand House","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":21.324156,"lo":-157.831206,"d":3934,"