onolulu, is the principal seaport of Honolulu and the State of Hawaiʻi in the United States. From the harbor, the City & County of Honolulu was developed and urbanized, in an outward fashion, over the course of the modern history of the island of Oahu. It includes Matson, Inc.","wpu":"Honolulu Harbor","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Flight_from_Honolulu_to_Hilo._Over_Sand_Island_and_Honolulu_%28503729%29_%2820897376415%29.jpg/960px-Flight_from_Honolulu_to_Hilo._Over_Sand_Island_and_Honolulu_%28503729%29_%2820897376415%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Hawaii Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.310556,"lo":-157.861111,"d":492,"ds":"The Hawaii Theatre is a theatre in downtown Honolulu, Hawaii, United States. Built in 1922, it is located at 1130 Bethel Street, between Hotel and Pauahi Streets, on the edge of Chinatown. It is listed on the State and National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Hawaii Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Hawaii-Theatre-daytime.JPG/960px-Hawaii-Theatre-daytime.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"C. Brewer Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.313056,"lo":-157.865833,"d":568,"ds":"The C. Brewer Building at 827 Fort Street in Honolulu, Hawaiʻi was built in 1930 to be the headquarters of C. Brewer & Co., the smallest of Hawaiʻi's Big Five corporations. The intimate, almost residential design was begun by Bertram Goodhue and completed by Hardie Phillip. The building was added to the National Register of Historic Places on 2 April 1980.","wpu":"C. Brewer Building","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/71/Cbrewerdoorway.JPG/960px-Cbrewerdoorway.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Cathedral Basilica of Our Lady of Peace","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.31075,"lo":-157.859417,"d":654,"ds":"The Cathedral Basilica of Our Lady of Peace is the mother church and cathedral of the Diocese of Honolulu.","wpu":"Cathedral Basilica of Our Lady of Peace","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c2/Cathedral_Basilica_of_Our_Lady_of_Peace_-_Honolulu_03.jpg/960px-Cathedral_Basilica_of_Our_Lady_of_Peace_-_Honolulu_03.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kawaiahaʻo Church","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.3043,"lo":-157.8579,"d":842,"ds":"Kawaiahaʻo Church is a historic Congregational church located in Downtown Honolulu on the Hawaiian Island of Oʻahu. The church, along with the Mission Houses, comprise the Hawaiian Mission Houses Historic Site, which was designated a U.S. National Historic Landmark (NHL) in 1962. In 1966 it and all other NHLs were included in the first issuance of the National Register of Historic Places.","wpu":"Kawaiahaʻo Church","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/ca/Kawaiaha%27o_Church.jpg/960px-Kawaiaha%27o_Church.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Izumo Taishakyo Mission of Hawaii","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.315551,"lo":-157.861023,"d":935,"ds":"The Izumo Taishakyo Mission is a Shinto shrine located in downtown Honolulu, Hawaii. It is one of the few active Shinto shrines in the United States. The wooden A-frame structure was inspired by Shimane Prefecture's classical Japanese shrine Izumo-taisha.","wpu":"Izumo Taishakyo Mission of Hawaii","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/4e/IZUMO_TAISHA-KYO_MISSION_01.jpg/960px-IZUMO_TAISHA-KYO_MISSION_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Mabel Smyth Memorial Building","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.306944,"lo":-157.855278,"d":1013,"ds":"The Mabel Smyth Memorial Building is a historic building in Honolulu, Hawaii. It was designed by Charles W. Dickey in 1937 and built in 1941. It was added to the National Register of Historic Places on February 3, 1994.","wpu":"Mabel Smyth Memorial Building","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d6/Honolulu-Mabel-Smyth-Auditorium.JPG/960px-Honolulu-Mabel-Smyth-Auditorium.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Honolulu molasses spill","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.3,"lo":-157.87,"d":1029,"ds":"In September 2013, 1,400 tons of molasses spilled into Honolulu Harbor in the U.S. state of Hawaii. The spill was discovered on September 9, 2013. It was caused by a faulty pipe that malfunctioned while the molasses was being loaded onto a ship, for which the shipping company Matson Navigation Co.","wpu":"Honolulu molasses spill","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/US_Coast_Guard_member_collecting_data_from_Honolulu_harbor.jpg/960px-US_Coast_Guard_member_collecting_data_from_Honolulu_harbor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sand Island (Hawaii)","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":21.306944,"lo":-157.879167,"d":1472,"ds":"Sand Island, formerly known as Quarantine Island, is a small island within the city of Honolulu, Hawaii, United States. The island lies at the entrance to Honolulu Harbor.","wpu":"Sand Island (Hawaii)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c5/A_storm_is_coming_-_looking_over_Sand_Island_%2812576824565%29.jpg/960px-A_storm_is_coming_-_looking_over_Sand_Island_%2812576824565%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"}],"about":{"ds":"Honolulu ( HON-ə-LOO-loo; Hawaiian: [honoˈlulu]) is the capital and most populous city of the U.S. state of Hawaii, located in the Pacific Ocean. It is the county seat of the consolidated City and County of Honolulu, situated along the southeast coast of the island of Oʻahu. The population of Honolulu was 350,964 at the 2020 census, dropping to an estimated 341,868 by 2025.","wpu":"Honolulu","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e8/2022_Views_from_Diamond_Head_02.jpg/960px-2022_Views_from_Diamond_Head_02.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}